{"name":"Madam Butterfly","hex":"#7ca7cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"madam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A polite form of address for a woman or lady.","The mistress of a household."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To address as \"madam\".","To be a madam; to run (a brothel)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/madam"},{"term":"butterfly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flying insect of the order Lepidoptera, distinguished from moths by their diurnal activity and generally brighter colouring. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa.","The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut (food) almost entirely in half and spread the halves apart, in a shape suggesting the wings of a butterfly.","To cut strips of surgical tape or plasters into thin strips, and place across (a gaping wound) to close it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/butterfly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Madam Butterfly (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Madam Butterfly \" is a song by English musician Malcolm McLaren, an electronic interpretation of the aria \"Un bel dì vedremo\" from Giacomo Puccini's opera Madama Butterfly. It was released as a single from McLaren's second studio album, Fans (1984), and reached number 13 on the UK Singles Chart and number 19 on the US Hot Dance/Disco chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/12/Malcolm_McLaren_-_Madam_Butterfly.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madam_Butterfly_(song)","wikidata":"Q65090172"},{"title":"Madam Butterfly (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Madam 